I"�<p>Imagine asking an AI agent to fix three bugs. One agent could tackle them in order, or three agents could work at once. The team can finish sooner if the fixes are independent. If two agents edit the same part of the code, they may spend much of that saved time sorting out their changes.</p>

<p>General Reasoning’s Avalon-M1 experiments ask whether AI agents can learn when it pays to work together. The researchers give the agents a shared score, tools for communicating, and a deadline. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/blog/how-to-train-a-swarm">General Reasoning’s How to Train a Swarm</a></p>

<!--more-->

<h2 id="what-a-deadline-teaches">What a deadline teaches</h2>

<p>In Avalon-M1, the lead agent and its specialists are instances of the same model. The lead can recruit specialists, and the team can share work through messages, channels, and a common workspace. During training, the outcome of the whole run shapes how the model learns from each agent’s actions. General Reasoning calls this <strong>reinforcement learning from collective rewards</strong>, or RLCR. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/avalonm1.pdf#page=9">Avalon-M1 technical report, methodology</a></p>

<p>Say one team passes seven tests before time runs out and another passes four. The first team earns a higher score. If both pass seven, they tie even if one finished earlier. The deadline makes speed matter when it helps the team complete more work. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/blog/how-to-train-a-swarm#making-parallel-work-pay">How to Train a Swarm, time limits</a></p>

<p>To make that incentive work, the task needs a way to score partial progress. A test suite can do this because it can check the code whenever a run stops. General Reasoning increases the time limit over training so teams can practice on tasks long enough for the work of dividing and coordinating a job to pay off. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/avalonm1.pdf#page=13">Avalon-M1 technical report, training horizons</a></p>

