<h2 id="when-parallel-work-pays">When parallel work pays</h2>

<p>Suppose each of the three fixes takes 18 minutes and the review takes 10. One agent working through them in order finishes in <strong>64 minutes</strong>. Three agents working independently can finish in <strong>28 minutes</strong>, including the review. If one fix depends on another, the review has to wait for both, and the schedule grows to <strong>46 minutes</strong>.</p>

<p>These are illustrative times. Each schedule contains the same amount of work. What changes is how much can happen at once. A quick message can save time if it warns someone away from duplicate work. A handoff can also add a delay if the next agent has to wait for an answer.</p>

<h2 id="what-avalon-m1-measured">What Avalon-M1 measured</h2>

<p>General Reasoning reports that, when the solo agent generated roughly as many output tokens as the team, its estimated run time was about four times as long. The researchers built that estimate from event logs, including the time to generate tokens, the time tools took to run, and which agents needed to wait for one another’s messages. Actual elapsed time also depends on how the serving system queues and batches requests. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/blog/how-to-train-a-swarm#signs-of-life">General Reasoning’s results</a> · <a href="https://www.gr.inc/avalonm1.pdf#page=59">Avalon-M1 technical report, latency methodology</a></p>

<p>Giving a solo agent the team’s token budget closes much of the score gap on some tasks. On others, the team or the solo agent still comes out ahead. The EdgeBench figure shows a different comparison. It tracks how scores change over the course of a run, including the team’s slower start. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/avalonm1.pdf#page=20">Avalon-M1 technical report, token comparison</a></p>

