<h2 id="what-a-team-score-can-miss">What a team score can miss</h2>

<p>Suppose one agent finds the cause of a bug, another repeats the same search, and a third catches an error in the patch. All three share the score. It tells the training system how the team did overall, with little detail about which actions made the difference. That becomes harder to untangle as teams grow and runs get longer. <a href="https://www.gr.inc/blog/how-to-train-a-swarm#outstanding-problems">General Reasoning’s open problems</a></p>

<p>For a bug-fixing task, I’d compare a solo agent with more time to think, several independent attempts, and a team that can talk. I’d give them the same deadline and track tests passed, tokens used, and time spent waiting for handoffs. The most interesting cases are the ones where one agent learns something that lets another finish sooner.</p>
